# Inspect Model Context

## Overview

Your support Agent answered, and you need to know what it actually sent to
the model: how many messages, which tools, how many tokens, whether the
prompt cache hit, and which provider request it was. Every model call
records two `Jido.AI.Context.Snapshot` documents, one before the call and
one after. By the end of this notebook you'll read them like this:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[first, second] = reply.data.model_attempts
before = Jido.AI.Context.Snapshot.decode!(second.context.before)

{before["stage"], before["shape"]["message_count"], before["shape"]["roles"]}
# => {"prepared", 4, %{"assistant" => 1, "system" => 1, "tool" => 1, "user" => 1}}
```

The whole notebook runs offline against a mocked provider.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

## Offline Model

ReqLLM sends its HTTP request through a `Req.Test` plug instead of the
network. These helpers build OpenAI Responses API bodies:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.FakeLLM do
  def options do
    %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]
    }
  end

  def tool_call(name, arguments, input_tokens, output_tokens) do
    response(
      "resp-tool",
      [
        %{
          "type" => "function_call",
          "id" => "fc-1",
          "call_id" => "call-1",
          "name" => name,
          "arguments" => Jason.encode!(arguments),
          "status" => "completed"
        }
      ],
      input_tokens,
      output_tokens,
      0
    )
  end

  def text(text, input_tokens, output_tokens, cached_tokens) do
    response(
      "resp-text",
      [
        %{
          "type" => "message",
          "id" => "msg-1",
          "role" => "assistant",
          "status" => "completed",
          "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
        }
      ],
      input_tokens,
      output_tokens,
      cached_tokens
    )
  end

  defp response(id, output, input_tokens, output_tokens, cached_tokens) do
    %{
      "id" => id,
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "output" => output,
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => input_tokens,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => cached_tokens},
        "output_tokens" => output_tokens,
        "total_tokens" => input_tokens + output_tokens
      }
    }
  end
end
```

## Define the Agent

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LookupOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "lookup_order",
    description: "Look up one order by ID.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, status: "shipped", eta: "2026-11-02"}}
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    id: "my_app/support_agent",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "support_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct

    tools do
      action MyApp.LookupOrder
    end
  end
end
```

## Run One Request

The provider answers twice: first with a `lookup_order` call, then with
text. The second response reports 64 cached input tokens:

```elixir
Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  Req.Test.json(conn, MyApp.FakeLLM.tool_call("lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"}, 120, 18))
end)

Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  Req.Test.json(conn, MyApp.FakeLLM.text("Order A-1001 arrives on 2026-11-02.", 180, 14, 64))
end)

agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-1")

signal =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is order A-1001?",
    options: MyApp.FakeLLM.options()
  })

{:ok, agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} = MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

{reply.data.status, reply.data.model_calls, reply.data.output}
```

The result is `{:completed, 2, "Order A-1001 arrives on 2026-11-02."}`.

## Find the Snapshots

`reply.data.model_attempts` has one entry per model call. Each one holds
two snapshots as JSON strings:

```elixir
[first, second] = reply.data.model_attempts

{first.context.format, is_binary(first.context.before), is_binary(first.context.after)}
```

The result is `{"jido.ai.context.snapshot+json", true, true}`.

The same attempts are committed to the Thread as `:ai_model_attempt`
entries, so you can read them later from `agent.state.session` without the
reply:

```elixir
agent.state.session.thread
|> Jido.Thread.filter_by_kind(:ai_model_attempt)
|> Enum.map(& &1.payload["context"]["format"])
```

The result is
`["jido.ai.context.snapshot+json", "jido.ai.context.snapshot+json"]`.

## Decode

`Jido.AI.Context.Snapshot.decode!/1` checks the `type` and `version` and
returns a map with string keys:

```elixir
alias Jido.AI.Context.Snapshot

before_1 = Snapshot.decode!(first.context.before)
after_1 = Snapshot.decode!(first.context.after)

{before_1["stage"], after_1["stage"], before_1["content"]}
```

The result is `{"prepared", "completed", "none"}`. The runtime stores
snapshots with `content: :none`, so no message text is kept.

Both snapshots describe one Context. They share its ID and request digest:

```elixir
{before_1["context_id"] == after_1["context_id"], before_1["digest"] == after_1["digest"]}
```

The result is `{true, true}`.

A document of another type fails:

```elixir
Snapshot.decode(~s({"type": "something.else", "version": 1}))
```

The result is `{:error, :invalid_context_snapshot}`.

## Compare Before and After

Only three top-level fields differ between the two snapshots of one call:

```elixir
before_1
|> Map.keys()
|> Enum.reject(&(before_1[&1] == after_1[&1]))
|> Enum.sort()
```

The result is `["captured_at_ms", "response", "stage"]`. The request,
shape, operations, and metadata are identical, which shows ReqLLM received
what was prepared. `response` is `nil` before the call.

## Compare Shapes

`shape` counts the request without its content. Compare the two model
calls:

```elixir
before_2 = Snapshot.decode!(second.context.before)

{before_1["shape"], before_2["shape"]}
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{%{
   "content_bytes" => 353,
   "content_part_count" => 2,
   "message_count" => 2,
   "reasoning_message_count" => 0,
   "roles" => %{"system" => 1, "user" => 1},
   "tool_count" => 1
 },
 %{
   "content_bytes" => 539,
   "content_part_count" => 3,
   "message_count" => 4,
   "reasoning_message_count" => 0,
   "roles" => %{"assistant" => 1, "system" => 1, "tool" => 1, "user" => 1},
   "tool_count" => 1
 }}
```

The second call added the assistant tool call and the tool result. The
assistant message has no text part, so `content_part_count` grows by one.

## Read the Messages

Each message summary keeps its role, a content digest, and its source:

```elixir
Enum.map(before_2["request"]["context"]["messages"], fn message ->
  {message["role"], message["metadata"]["jido"]["source"]["type"], Map.has_key?(message, "content")}
end)
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  {"system", "agent_instructions", false},
  {"user", "thread_entry", false},
  {"assistant", "thread_entry", false},
  {"tool", "thread_entry", false}
]
```

`"agent_instructions"` is the Flow's `instructions`. `"thread_entry"`
messages came from the Thread; their source also holds the entry `seq`
and refs. `content_digest` lets you check that two calls sent the same
text without storing it.

Tools and options are summarized the same way. Credentials are replaced:

```elixir
options = before_1["request"]["options"]

{before_1["request"]["model"], Enum.map(options["tools"], & &1["name"]), options["api_key"]}
```

The result is `{"openai:gpt-4o-mini", ["lookup_order"], "[REDACTED]"}`.

## Projection Facts

The Context metadata records which Thread revision the call was built
from and how the projection chose messages:

```elixir
projection = before_2["metadata"]["projection"]

{before_2["metadata"]["source"]["thread_rev"], projection["included_messages"],
 projection["dropped_messages"], projection["limits"]}
```

The result is
`{5, 3, 0, %{"max_bytes" => 200000, "max_messages" => 64}}`. The Thread
had five entries: the user message, the first `:ai_model_attempt`, the
assistant tool call, an `:ai_tool_attempt`, and the tool result. Three are
messages, and all three were included. The system message isn't from the
Thread.
[Project a Thread](project-a-thread.livemd) explains each decision.

## Token Counts

The after snapshot keeps ReqLLM's normalized usage for that call:

```elixir
after_2 = Snapshot.decode!(second.context.after)

for snapshot <- [after_1, after_2] do
  usage = snapshot["response"]["call_metadata"]["usage"]
  {usage["input_tokens"], usage["output_tokens"], usage["cached_tokens"]}
end
```

The result is `[{120, 18, 0}, {180, 14, 64}]`. The reply sums them:

```elixir
Map.take(reply.data.usage, [:input_tokens, :output_tokens, :cached_tokens, :total_tokens])
```

The result is
`%{cached_tokens: 64, input_tokens: 300, output_tokens: 32, total_tokens: 332}`.

## Cache Facts

Each attempt also records what the call observed about the prompt cache:

```elixir
for attempt <- [first, second] do
  Map.take(attempt.prompt_cache, [:requested, :read_tokens, :write_tokens])
end
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  %{requested: false, read_tokens: 0, write_tokens: 0},
  %{requested: false, read_tokens: 64, write_tokens: 0}
]
```

`requested` is `false` because this request didn't set `prompt_cache`.
The provider can still report cached tokens.
[Usage, Cache, and Cost](usage-cache-and-cost.md) covers cache requests
and cost data.

## Request IDs

Four IDs connect a call to everything else:

```elixir
call = before_1["metadata"]["call"]
call_metadata = after_1["response"]["call_metadata"]

%{
  request_id: call["request_id"] == reply.data.request_id,
  run_id: call["run_id"] == reply.data.run_id,
  model_call: call["model_call"],
  req_llm_request_id: call_metadata["request_id"] == first.req_llm_request_id
}
```

The result is
`%{model_call: 1, request_id: true, req_llm_request_id: true, run_id: true}`.

| ID | Identifies |
| --- | --- |
| `request_id` | The request Signal. Also in every Thread entry's refs. |
| `run_id` | One request run. Also in every Thread entry's refs. |
| `model_call` | The call's position in the request, from 1. |
| `req_llm_request_id` | The ReqLLM request. Use it to match ReqLLM telemetry. |

A failed call has no response body, so the runtime copies the ReqLLM
request ID into `response.call_metadata.request_id` from completion
metadata.

## Provider Metadata

`call_metadata` also holds the provider's own response facts:

```elixir
Map.take(call_metadata, ["model", "response_id", "finish_reason"])
```

The result is
`%{"finish_reason" => "tool_calls", "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "response_id" => "resp-tool"}`.

```elixir
Map.take(call_metadata["provider_metadata"], ["api_type", "status"])
```

The result is `%{"api_type" => "responses", "status" => "completed"}`.
`model` here is the provider's resolved model, while
`request.model` is the model you asked for.

## Summary

- Each entry in `reply.data.model_attempts` and each `:ai_model_attempt`
  Thread entry holds `context.before` and `context.after` JSON snapshots.
- Decode them with `Jido.AI.Context.Snapshot.decode!/1`. Keys are strings.
- `shape` gives message, tool, role, part, and byte counts without content.
- Usage and provider facts are in `response.call_metadata` of the after
  snapshot. Cache observations are in the attempt's `prompt_cache`.
- `request_id`, `run_id`, and `model_call` in `metadata.call` tie the
  snapshot to the request and the Thread.

